Use Postgres full-text search, which you already run. At 38,000 rows growing by a few hundred a month, a separate search service like Elasticsearch isn't justified.

**Schema.** Add a generated, weighted `tsvector` column and a GIN index on it:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search tsvector GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
) STORED;

CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY products_search_idx ON products USING gin (search);
```

The generated column keeps the index in sync with no triggers or application code. Name matches weigh more than description matches.

**Route.** Add `GET /products/search?q=` in `routes/products.js`. Declare it before `/:id`, or Express will treat "search" as an id.

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents
       FROM products, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1) query
      WHERE active AND search @@ query
      ORDER BY ts_rank(search, query) DESC, name
      LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

`websearch_to_tsquery` accepts raw user input without throwing on odd punctuation, so you don't need to sanitize the query yourself.

**Decision that forks this:** do users need typo tolerance or matching while they type? Full-text search matches whole stemmed words, so "wireles" or a half-typed last word returns nothing. If yes, add `pg_trgm` with a trigram index on `name` and fall back to it when full-text returns few results. If the box is submitted on Enter and users rarely misspell product names, the above is enough.

The `english` text search configuration is an assumption. If product names include brand names or SKUs in other languages, the `simple` configuration is safer.

